{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## RECURRENCE — and this time the root cause is identified, not just the symptom\n\nreview-bot went down again and stayed down ~59 minutes, silently. I restored it manually. But the\nimportant part is *why* it did not come back on its own, which this ticket previously did not have.\n\n### Root cause: `unless-stopped` plus a clean exit is a permanent outage\n\n```\ndocker inspect -f '{{.HostConfig.RestartPolicy.Name}}':\n  compose-review-bot-1   unless-stopped\n  compose-opcert-1       unless-stopped\n  compose-gerrit-1       unless-stopped\n\ndocker ps -a  (before my intervention):\n  compose-review-bot-1 | Exited (0) 59 minutes ago      <-- clean exit, code 0\n  rebar-mcp-...        | Up About a minute (healthy)\n  compose-opcert-1     | Up About a minute\n  compose-gerrit-1     | Up About a minute\n```\n\n`unless-stopped` means \"restart it unless it was **explicitly stopped**.\" review-bot exited with\n**code 0** — a clean, voluntary exit — and Docker therefore treated it as intentionally stopped and\ndeclined to restart it, including across the host reboot that brought every other container back.\n\nSo this is not a flaky container or a crash loop. It is a policy that is *correct* for a service a\nhuman stops on purpose, and *wrong* for a service whose absence silently disables the merge gate.\nThe other three containers came back precisely because they had not exited cleanly first.\n\n**Consequence, stated plainly: with review-bot down there is no source of `LLM-Review` votes, so no\nchange can satisfy the two-vote gate and nothing can merge.** For 59 minutes the project's merge path\nwas closed and nothing said so.\n\n### The reboot was deliberate and external — not a crash\n\nWorth recording so this is not misfiled as instability:\n\n```\nSep 06 02:40:41 systemd-logind: Power key pressed short.\nSep 06 02:40:42 systemd-logind: Powering off...\nSep 06 02:41:11 systemd-logind: Failed to get load state of poweroff.target: Connection timed out\nSep 06 02:41:12 systemd-logind: Failed to execute poweroff operation: Connection timed out\nSep 06 02:41:16 systemd-logind: Power key pressed short.        <-- second press needed\n       host back up 02:43,  `uptime` = 0 min,  EC2 status checks ok throughout\n```\n\nAn ACPI power-button event (an EC2 API reboot), not an OOM or panic. Note the shutdown itself\n**hung** and required a second press — consistent with the loaded host, and worth remembering as a\nsymptom to expect during future maintenance.\n\nDuring the window Gerrit was fully unreachable at TCP level (`HTTP=000`, connect timeout at 45s and\n100s; `git ls-remote` killed at 90s). It recovered on its own: root now answers **HTTP 200 in 0.50s**\nand `ls-remote` returns in **2s**.\n\n### Two incidental findings from the same logs\n\n1. **Probe truncation confirmed from the host's own mouth**, corroborating the metric-side analysis:\n   `rebar-health: probe exit report: SERVICE_RESULT=timeout EXIT_CODE=killed EXIT_STATUS=TERM\n   probe_truncated=1`.\n2. **A duplicate `Change-Id` is breaking the review bot for one change.** Its reconcile loop logs:\n   `HTTP 404: Multiple changes found for I266870f28c4935ad009ceff3d9c32479cb2c7988`. That is the\n   failure mode `git commit --amend -m/-F` produces (it drops the trailer, the hook stamps a fresh\n   one, and a duplicate change opens). The bot cannot resolve the id, so it cannot vote on that\n   change — worth its own ticket if it is not already tracked.\n\n### Suggested direction (not a decision)\n\n`unless-stopped` -> `always` for review-bot would fix the specific gap, but on its own it converts a\nsilent outage into a possible crash loop, so it needs pairing with an actual liveness check on the\n*vote-casting capability* rather than on the process. The deeper issue this ticket already names is\nthat autodeploy reported \"up to date\" while the service was down: a deploy check that inspects\ndesired-vs-actual image state, and not whether the container is running and voting, will keep\nreporting green through exactly this failure."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788662864776870001,"uuid":"3e3d48a6-51e3-420d-b082-f247e88c7fc3"}